WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.700
Quality, fair apprentices, and the five numbers to track.

2
00:00:04.050 --> 00:00:08.170
This is the video for module 3 of Fashion Business Management.

3
00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:16.220
One badly fitted outfit at a wedding is seen by hundreds of guests, and the story travels faster than any advert.

4
00:00:16.570 --> 00:00:19.690
For your client, quality means three things. It fits.

5
00:00:20.940 --> 00:00:24.090
It is well finished. And it is ready when you promised.

6
00:00:25.340 --> 00:00:33.410
In this module you also learn to manage apprentices fairly and safely, and to measure whether your business is truly improving.

7
00:00:34.660 --> 00:00:44.220
Most problems have a simple cause. Wrong or old measurements. Cotton not pre-washed. Wrong tension or a blunt needle. Too many orders accepted.

8
00:00:45.470 --> 00:00:53.890
So measure in a fixed order and date it. Offer to pre-wash cotton such as some Ankara. Test stitch on an offcut and press each seam.

9
00:00:54.240 --> 00:01:04.420
And never skip the fitting. Before finishing, a seam is easy to adjust. After overlocking and pressing, the same change takes longer and can leave marks.

10
00:01:05.670 --> 00:01:15.780
Turn the garment inside out and look at every seam. Try every zip and hook. Measure the bust, waist and length against the order page, then press and photograph it.

11
00:01:16.130 --> 00:01:21.660
If an apprentice sewed it, the owner or senior tailor does this check, not the person who sewed it.

12
00:01:22.910 --> 00:01:32.480
For example, Ronke in Lagos had late outfits last December, and two clients left. With one trained helper, she can finish about 7 gowns a week.

13
00:01:33.730 --> 00:01:42.010
In October she announces that December bookings close when 21 slots are filled, or on 30 November, whichever comes first.

14
00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:47.420
She does not hold slots for promises. No deposit, no slot.

15
00:01:48.670 --> 00:01:56.050
She books fittings early and closes at 20 slots, keeping one free, because power cuts, illness and breakdowns happen.

16
00:01:56.400 --> 00:02:02.290
She delivers every order on time, and her clients return in January because they trust her dates.

17
00:02:03.540 --> 00:02:13.700
Many disputes start with word-of-mouth terms. Write a one-page agreement, read it aloud with the apprentice and a parent or guardian, and give each side a copy.

18
00:02:14.950 --> 00:02:20.790
Start her on offcuts and keep a skills record with dates. It shows her progress and protects you.

19
00:02:22.040 --> 00:02:32.540
Beating, insults and threats are abuse and have no place in a workshop. Teach safety on day one. New learners use the iron and machines only under supervision.

20
00:02:32.890 --> 00:02:37.150
If a socket sparks, switch off at the main and do not touch the appliance.

21
00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:46.470
You can pay a trained helper a fixed wage, or per piece. Per-piece pay suits busy seasons, but you must check quality closely.

22
00:02:47.720 --> 00:03:00.110
Grace's gown price already has 8,000 naira of labour, for 8 hours. Uche sews the main construction in about 5 hours, so Grace pays 5,000 naira per gown sewn to standard.

23
00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:12.920
Uche sews 10 gowns and earns 50,000 naira. One needs rework, which Uche fixes before payment. Grace's own share stays in the price, so her margin is protected.

24
00:03:14.170 --> 00:03:21.830
Track five numbers monthly: sales, profit, on-time delivery rate, repeat client rate, and average order value.

25
00:03:22.180 --> 00:03:29.240
For example, Aisha in Kano delivered 14 of 20 orders on time in April. That is 70%.

26
00:03:30.490 --> 00:03:45.640
In May she capped weekly bookings and added fittings four days before due dates. On-time delivery rose to about 94%, repeat clients rose, and profit grew by about 30,000 naira, even with fewer orders.

27
00:03:46.890 --> 00:03:55.000
Write a one-page report. A headline, your five numbers side by side, and what went well, with evidence such as a client review.

28
00:03:56.250 --> 00:04:03.650
Be honest about what went wrong. Then choose one action only. If you change everything at once, you cannot tell what worked.

29
00:04:04.900 --> 00:04:16.630
To remember. Check every garment before it leaves. Book within your capacity. Treat apprentices fairly and keep them safe. And let your numbers, not your busy feeling, guide your next step.

30
00:04:17.880 --> 00:04:26.800
Your turn. Run the checklist on your next three garments, write your alteration policy, draft your apprenticeship pack, and write your first report.

31
00:04:27.150 --> 00:04:29.090
Continue with the lessons below.
